  <meta name="theme-color" content="#174f57">
  <title>Hubuum — $name / Design study $number</title>
  <link rel="icon" href="assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/site.css">
  <script src="assets/site.js" defer></script>
</head>
<body class="concept $slug">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
  <aside class="review-strip" aria-label="Design review">
    <a href="index.html">← <span>All concepts</span></a>
    <span class="review-label">Landing page studies</span>
    <nav aria-label="Choose a design direction">$switcher</nav>
  </aside>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="container header-inner">
      <a class="brand" href="#main" aria-label="Hubuum home">$symbol<span>hubuum</span></a>
      <nav class="main-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="#idea">The CMDB</a><a href="#structure">Your model</a><a href="#ecosystem">Ecosystem</a>
      </nav>
      <a class="header-cta" href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum/">Documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="container hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow"><span class="status-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span> The flexible, open-source CMDB</p>
          <h1>$headline</h1>
          <p class="hero-description">$description</p>
          <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="https://hubuum.github.io/get-started/">Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button quiet" href="#idea">Discover Hubuum <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
        </div>
        <figure class="hero-art"><img src="assets/$slug.svg" alt="$idea" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>$caption</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="container hero-foot"><span>Schema-bound or schema-free. Connected by design.</span><a href="#idea">Explore your CMDB <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter introduction" id="idea">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label"><span>01 / Your configuration management database</span><span class="section-rule"></span><span>Yours to shape.</span></div>
        <div class="intro-grid">
          <h2>A place for the data<br><em>that matters.</em></h2>
          <div class="section-copy"><p class="lead">Assets, applications, services, locations.<br>And the things only your organisation manages.</p><p>Hubuum is a configuration management database (CMDB) whose content and resource types you define. Store JSON data about anything you manage, in classes that reflect your world.</p><p>Build an inventory around your organisation, with room for both established configuration and information that is still taking shape.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="resource-types" aria-label="Examples of resource types you can define"><span>Services</span><span>Infrastructure</span><span>Configuration</span><span>Organisational data</span><span class="custom-type">Your own concepts <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter authority-section" id="authority">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label"><span>02 / Authority where it belongs</span><span class="section-rule"></span><span>Different sources. A shared inventory.</span></div>
        <div class="authority-heading"><h2>Authoritative here.<br>Authoritative elsewhere.<br><em>Together in Hubuum.</em></h2><p>Manage data you own alongside reference data whose source of truth lives in another system.</p></div>
        <div class="authority-grid">
          <article class="authority-card"><span class="eyebrow">Data you maintain in Hubuum</span><h3>Make it the source of truth.</h3><p>Use Hubuum as the authoritative home for records you manage directly. Your service catalogue, organisational information, or domain-specific inventory can start here.</p><div class="authority-example"><span class="example-symbol" aria-hidden="true">$symbol</span><div><span class="example-label">Example / service catalogue</span><strong>Atlas</strong><span>Maintained in Hubuum</span></div></div></article>
          <article class="authority-card"><span class="eyebrow">Data you bring from other systems</span><h3>Keep its authority upstream.</h3><p>Hold non-authoritative reference data alongside your own records. Your integrations decide what to collect and how to refresh it from the systems that own it.</p><div class="authority-example"><span class="example-symbol source-symbol" aria-hidden="true">↳</span><div><span class="example-label">Example / server inventory</span><strong>web-01</strong><span>Maintained in an existing inventory system</span></div></div></article>
        </div>
        <div class="authority-bridge"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>$symbol<strong>One CMDB. Both kinds of data.</strong><span aria-hidden="true"></span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter structure-section" id="structure">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label"><span>03 / Choose the structure</span><span class="section-rule"></span><span>Bound or free. Side by side.</span></div>
        <div class="structure-heading"><h2>Structure where you need it.<br><em>Freedom where you don’t.</em></h2><p>A class can use JSON Schema to validate its objects, or keep their data schema-free. Choose the structure each kind of resource needs.</p></div>
        <div class="schema-grid">
          <article class="schema-card schema-bound"><div class="schema-card-heading"><span class="schema-glyph" aria-hidden="true">▦</span><div><span class="eyebrow">Schema-bound</span><h3>A shape you can rely on.</h3></div></div><p>Define a schema and require validation for predictable, consistent records.</p><div class="record-example"><div class="record-title"><strong>Service / Atlas</strong><span>Example data</span></div><dl><div><dt>owner <span>required string</span></dt><dd>Platform</dd></div><div><dt>tier <span>required integer</span></dt><dd>1</dd></div><div><dt>environment <span>required string</span></dt><dd>production</dd></div></dl></div><span class="schema-caption">Explicit expectations. Validated objects.</span></article>
          <article class="schema-card schema-free"><div class="schema-card-heading"><span class="schema-glyph" aria-hidden="true">{ }</span><div><span class="eyebrow">Schema-free</span><h3>Room for what comes next.</h3></div></div><p>Keep JSON data flexible as ideas develop and the shape of your information changes.</p><div class="record-example"><div class="record-title"><strong>Context / Research notes</strong><span>Example data</span></div><pre><code>{
  "purpose": "Explore new hosting",
  "questions": ["Capacity?", "Location?"],
  "candidate": true
}</code></pre></div><span class="schema-caption">Open-ended content. A model that can evolve.</span></article>
        </div>
        <p class="schema-common">Both can hold <strong>authoritative records</strong> or <strong>reference data</strong>.<br>Where data is owned and how it is structured are independent choices.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter connection-section" id="connections">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label"><span>04 / Bind objects through relations</span><span class="section-rule"></span><span>Make the context useful.</span></div>
        <div class="connection-heading"><h2>Objects hold the facts.<br><em>Relationships provide<br>the context.</em></h2><p>Connect objects across resource types,<br>sources, and schema choices.</p></div>
        <div class="connection-grid">
          <div class="model-figure"><img src="assets/relationships.svg" loading="lazy" alt="Example: schema-bound service Atlas, authoritative in Hubuum, runs on schema-bound server web-01 from an upstream source. Atlas is described by schema-free research notes owned in Hubuum. The server is located in Oslo, represented by schema-free reference data from upstream."><span class="figure-caption">Example model · authority and schema choices can vary independently</span></div>
          <div class="model-story">
            <div class="model-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explore the model">
              <button type="button" id="tab-model" role="tab" aria-controls="panel-model" aria-selected="true">Objects</button>
              <button type="button" id="tab-connect" role="tab" aria-controls="panel-connect" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Relations</button>
              <button type="button" id="tab-use" role="tab" aria-controls="panel-use" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Access</button>
            </div>
            <div id="panel-model" class="model-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-model" tabindex="0"><span class="eyebrow">Different content. A common model.</span><h3>A place for every<br>kind of resource.</h3><p>Each object belongs to a class you define. Its JSON data can follow a required schema or remain schema-free. Both kinds participate in the same CMDB.</p><div class="example-detail"><span>Service</span><strong>Atlas · schema-bound</strong><span>Context</span><strong>Research notes · schema-free</strong><span>Connection</span><strong>Atlas is described by the notes</strong></div></div>
            <div id="panel-connect" class="model-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-connect" tabindex="0" hidden><span class="eyebrow">Facts become context</span><h3>Bring the objects<br>into relation.</h3><p>Connect a service you maintain in Hubuum to server records from another inventory. Link configuration to flexible context. Build the relationships that matter to your organisation.</p><div class="example-detail"><span>Service</span><strong>Atlas</strong><span>Runs on</span><strong>web-01</strong><span>Located in</span><strong>Oslo</strong></div></div>
            <div id="panel-use" class="model-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-use" tabindex="0" hidden><span class="eyebrow">A shared model. Intentional access.</span><h3>Give people the<br>access they need.</h3><p>Organise resources in collections and control access through group permissions. Give people and service accounts responsibilities that fit how you work.</p><div class="example-detail"><span>Organise</span><strong>Collections</strong><span>People</span><strong>Users and groups</strong><span>Automation</span><strong>Service accounts</strong></div></div>
            <a class="text-link" href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum/">Explore the server documentation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter ecosystem-section" id="ecosystem">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="section-label"><span>05 / One connected ecosystem</span><span class="section-rule"></span><span>Choose your way in.</span></div>
        <div class="ecosystem-heading"><h2>One CMDB.<br><em>Many ways to work.</em></h2><p>Search, import, export, and build on a consistent API.<br>Explore your data and follow how it changes.</p></div>
        <div class="ecosystem-grid">
          <a class="ecosystem-server" href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum/"><span class="eyebrow">The foundation</span>$symbol<h3>Hubuum Server</h3><p>Your CMDB: flexible data, optional schemas, relationships, permissions, and a shared API.</p><span class="text-link">Deploy and explore <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></span></a>
          <div class="ecosystem-tools">
            <a href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum-frontend/"><span class="tool-icon" aria-hidden="true">▦</span><div><h3>Web frontend</h3><p>A window into your inventory.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum-cli/"><span class="tool-icon" aria-hidden="true">⌘</span><div><h3>Command line</h3><p>Explore, script, and automate.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum-client-rust/"><span class="tool-icon code-icon" aria-hidden="true">Rs</span><div><h3>Rust client</h3><p>Typed access for your applications.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
            <a href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum-client-python/"><span class="tool-icon code-icon" aria-hidden="true">Py</span><div><h3>Python client</h3><p>Connect your Python workflows.</p></div><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closing">
      <div class="container closing-inner"><div><p class="eyebrow">Open source. Self-hosted. Yours to shape.</p><h2>Your data belongs.<br><em>Give it a model.</em></h2><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href="https://hubuum.github.io/get-started/">Get started <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button quiet" href="https://github.com/hubuum/hubuum">Explore on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div><img src="assets/$slug.svg" loading="lazy" alt="" class="closing-art"></div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-inner"><div><a class="brand" href="#main">$symbol<span>hubuum</span></a><p>A flexible CMDB for your data, on your terms.</p></div><div class="footer-meta"><nav aria-label="Footer"><a href="https://hubuum.github.io/hubuum/">Documentation</a><a href="https://github.com/hubuum">GitHub</a><a href="https://github.com/hubuum/hubuum/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT licence</a></nav><p>Open source · Pre-1.0 · For evaluation and early deployments</p></div></div></footer>
</body>
</html>
